WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.550
Sensitive data often exists in more places than the primary database.

2
00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:17.122
It may appear in object storage, generated files, caches, queues, event streams, dead-letter stores, backups, snapshots, search indexes, analytics stores, exports, and support downloads.

3
00:00:17.122 --> 00:00:19.899
Each copy extends the protection problem.

4
00:00:19.899 --> 00:00:30.773
Databases need least-privilege access, schema review, tenant-boundary enforcement, encryption decisions, backup handling, and administrative access control.

5
00:00:30.773 --> 00:00:36.866
Application accounts should have the permissions they need, not broad ownership by default.

6
00:00:36.866 --> 00:00:43.191
Tenant and customer boundaries should be enforced by trusted server-side logic and data design.

7
00:00:43.191 --> 00:00:46.276
Files and object storage need their own review.

8
00:00:46.276 --> 00:00:58.153
Names, metadata, folder structure, access control, retention, content safety scanning workflow, download rules, and separation from executable application content all matter.

9
00:00:58.153 --> 00:01:03.860
A file export may contain more sensitive information than the screen that generated it.

10
00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:12.653
Caches can expose sensitive data if entries are too broad, too long-lived, shared across tenants, or visible to unintended operators.

11
00:01:12.653 --> 00:01:24.607
Queues and event streams should avoid unnecessary sensitive fields because messages may be copied, retried, retained, dead-lettered, inspected, or consumed by multiple services.

12
00:01:24.607 --> 00:01:34.479
Backups, snapshots, exports, search indexes, analytics stores, and data lakes need the same classification and retention review as production data.

13
00:01:34.479 --> 00:01:45.894
Deletion and retention requirements should account for copies, derived data, operational stores, and restore procedures, not just the original row in the primary database.
